INT. FERRIS WHEEL GONDOLA - STOPPED AT THE TOP - NIGHT

A wind-rocked cabin sixty metres over a closed fairground. Painted bulbs flicker on the rim below. A duffel sits on the bench.

LYSANDER POTTS (30s, tool belt over a ride-attendant's vest) kneels on the cabin floor with a drill, boring slowly through the planks. He hums under his breath. The drill whines, chews, drops. A fist-sized hole opens, framing the dark ticket booth below and a faint silver glint inside it.

He lowers a line through the hole, clips a harness to the rail, and tests the knot with a bounce. The gondola creaks. He pauses. Something else is creaking.

A gloved hand grips the outside of the cage. Then another. A woman hauls herself up the bars and hooks one elbow through the cabin door: IMOGEN FAIRBANKS-DUBOIS (30s, ski mask rolled up like a hat, grin like a crescent moon, breathing hard).

IMOGEN
Evening, Lysander.

Lysander lunges for the duffel. Imogen swings in, kicks the drill across the floor, and catches him in a bear hug. They wrestle, the cabin rocking, bulbs flaring and dying as they slam against the cage. His elbow cracks into the bench. Her boot catches the rail. The line goes taut and flaps across both of them.

Then both freeze. Below, in the dark fairground, a torch beam swings across the ticket booth.

LYSANDER
(whispering)
Is that yours?

IMOGEN
(whispering)
I thought it was yours.

The beam steadies on the hole in the floor.

Lysander slowly lets go. Imogen unhooks her elbow. Neither lifts their hands.

FADE OUT.
